13: 
14: ### 4.6 A worked white-dwarf calculation: a transient retarded scattering signal and orbital-timescale bounds
15: 
16: **Imported from prior work.** Spectroscopy gives the inner white dwarf of PSR J0337+1715 \(T_{\rm eff}=15{,}800\pm100\) K, \(\log g=5.82\pm0.05\), \(R=0.091\pm0.005\,R_\odot\) and a hydrogen (DA) atmosphere \cite{kaplan2014j0337}. Masses and semimajor axes below are those of the Section 5.9 parameter set (inner white dwarf \(0.19754\,M_\odot\)). Orbital eccentricities are from \cite{ransom2014triple}.
17: 
18: **Counterexample candidate.** We examine one internal state of this star: the scalar-driven displacement of its own matter. From Section 4.3 we reuse only \(a_i\), \(a_o\) and \(a_p\), the charges per unit mass of the inner white dwarf, the outer white dwarf and the pulsar. All other symbols are local to this section.
19: 
20: - **Theory.** The declared theory has one massless scalar with \(A(\varphi)=e^{\beta_s\varphi^2/2}\) and \(\beta_s=-4\), so the matter coupling is \(\alpha_s(\varphi)=\beta_s\varphi\). The asymptotic value \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\) is a linearization point, and \(\alpha_0=\alpha_s(\varphi_\infty)\).
21: - **Background.** The static background metric is \(ds^2=-\mathcal N^2c^2dt^2+\mathcal L^2dr^2+r^2d\Omega^2\). The optical coordinate is \(x=-\int_r^{R_d}(\mathcal L/\mathcal N)\,dr\), with \(x'=dx/dr\). The background scalar is \(\varphi_0(r)\), the density \(\rho_0(r)\), and \(T_0\) is the trace of the background matter stress-energy tensor. The field variable is \(\Psi=r\,\delta\varphi\).
22: - **Masses.** \(M_{\rm wd}\) is the white dwarf's mass and \(m_{\rm wd}=GM_{\rm wd}/c^2\) its geometric mass.
23: 
24: **Counterexample candidate.** The background is a static weak-field model with a helium core and a hydrogen-rich envelope (\(X\simeq0.89\)). It has \(M_{\rm wd}=0.198\,M_\odot\), \(R=6.91\times10^9\) cm, \(T_{\rm eff}=15{,}898\) K and \(\log g=5.740\), with a gray Eddington envelope built with Rosseland tables. An ingoing pulse drives the coupled evolution of baryons, gas, photon transport and one returned GR metric until \(t_e=2t_p\):
25: \[
26: \Psi_{\rm in}=\eta R_d\,\mathcal P\big((t+x/c)/t_p\big),\qquad \mathcal P(s)=(4s(1-s))^4\ (0<s<1),\ 0\ \text{otherwise},
27: \]
28: with \(t_p=1.717\) ms, \(\eta=10^{-30}\) and \(R_d=6.91\times10^9\) cm. The readout at \(R_d\) is
29: \[
30: \mathcal Q=-(\Psi_{\rm out}+\alpha_0\,\delta m_{\rm wd})/m_{\rm wd},
31: \]
32: the change of \(a_i\) inferred from the exterior monopole after removing the contribution of the exterior mass change \(\delta m_{\rm wd}\). It excludes the instantaneous polarization \(\beta_sT_0\delta\varphi\), which belongs to the wave operator. The pulse length \(ct_p\simeq515\) km is far below \(R\), so this is a finite-size response outside the point-particle regime \(\lambda\gg R\).
33: 
34: **Imported from prior work.** For orientation only, since the two numbers use different norms: the Born scattering produced by the instantaneous polarization reaches \(1.7\times10^{-10}\) of the incident field as a maximum over the stored space–time array of the coupled calculation, while the endpoint readout below corresponds to an outgoing amplitude of about \(10^{-26}\) of the incident field at \(R_d\).
35: 
36: **Imported from prior work.** At the protocol endpoint the readout is negative. We extrapolate the compact part \(-\Psi_{\rm out}/m_{\rm wd}\) over interior refinements by 1, 2 and 4 with the Richardson method (observed order 2.00; change from 2\(\times\) to 4\(\times\) of \(-1.2\%\)). The mass term, measured on the unrefined solution, multiplies it by \(1+2.2\times10^{-5}\). The result is
37: 
38: \begin{equation}
39: \mathcal Q(t_e)=-2.3\times10^{-51}.
40: \label{eq:wd-readout}
41: \end{equation}
42: 
43: Its composition is as follows.
44: 
45: - The direct part, the instantaneous product of the pulse and the background geometry, contributes \(10^{-8}\) of it.
46: - The rest-mass redistribution of baryons contributes 0.999996, and 99.9% comes from layers the pulse has already left.
47: - With the background fixed, disabling the Planck–Larkin occupation probabilities in the equation of state and opacities changes it by at most a relative \(2.2\times10^{-10}\).
48: 
49: **Conjectural.** With the \(\varphi_\infty^2\) scaling discussed below, Equation (\ref{eq:wd-readout}) corresponds to \(\mathcal Q(t_e)/(\eta\varphi_\infty^2)\simeq-2.3\times10^{-15}\).
50: 
51: **Proven.** For pressureless matter at linear order, the radial displacement
52: 
53: \begin{equation}
54: \xi=4c^2\frac{\mathcal N^2}{\mathcal L^2}\eta R_dt_p^2\Big[\Big(\frac{\varphi_0'}{r}-\frac{\varphi_0}{r^2}\Big)F_2(p)+\frac{\varphi_0x'}{rct_p}F_1(p)\Big],\qquad p=\frac{t+x/c}{t_p},
55: \label{eq:free-fall}
56: \end{equation}
57: 
58: with \(F_1=\int_0^p\mathcal P\) and \(F_2=\int_0^pF_1\), solves \(\ddot\xi=-c^2(\mathcal N^2/\mathcal L^2)\partial_r[\alpha_s(\varphi_0)\delta\varphi]\), with \(\xi=\dot\xi=0\) before the pulse arrives. **Imported from prior work.** The resulting face mass fluxes \(4\pi r^2\mathcal L\rho_0\xi\), passed through the retarded propagator of the coupled calculation, reproduce its continuum limit to \(5.2\times10^{-5}\).
59: 
60: **Proven.** In flat space the scattered outgoing monopole of spherical shells depends only on the retarded time \(u=t-r/c\). A shell at radius \(r_e\) carrying the mass perturbation \(\delta M_e(t)\) contributes
61: 
62: \begin{equation}
63: \mathcal Q(u)=\frac{1}{M_{\rm wd}}\sum_e w_e\,\frac{c}{2r_e}\int_{u-r_e/c}^{u+r_e/c}\delta M_e(t')\,dt',\qquad w=\alpha_s(\varphi_0)\,\mathcal N,
64: \label{eq:wd-window}
65: \end{equation}
66: 
67: whose static limit is \(\sum_ew_e\delta M_e/M_{\rm wd}\). A mass-conserving redistribution therefore contributes only in two ways: through the radial variation of \(w\), and through light-crossing windows that are not yet complete.
68: 
69: **Imported from prior work.** We also evolved a whole-star linear adiabatic radial model with Newtonian self-gravity. It is driven by the scalar force behind Equation (\ref{eq:free-fall}), including the regular pulse reflected through the centre.
70: 
71: - It reproduces the endpoint readout to \(-0.81\%\) and the free-fall displacement in the charge layers to \(1.2\times10^{-6}\).
72: - In this model the static-window value at \(t_e\) is \(+3.6\times10^{-54}\), of opposite sign and 1/640 of the magnitude.
73: - The readout rises steeply after \(t_e\): \(-2.8\times10^{-51}\) at 3.5 ms and \(-8.7\times10^{-51}\) at 4.0 ms.
74: - In source time, the pulse reaches the centre at 0.2305 s and leaves the star at 0.4627 s.
75: 
76: **Imported from prior work.** In readout time \(t_R\) at \(R_d\), the model gives the following history. Values come from the stored samples (0.25 ms apart up to 20 ms, 2 ms apart after); the peak comes from the run's 0.2 ms grid.
77: 
78: - The readout stays negative until \(t_R\simeq0.43\) s. It grows from \(-2.3\times10^{-51}\) at \(t_e\) to \(-3.0\times10^{-43}\) at 0.1 s, \(-8.0\times10^{-41}\) at 0.23 s and \(-2.7\times10^{-39}\) at 0.35 s.
79: - The signals from the centre arrival and from the outgoing pass reach the readout together near \(2R_d/c\simeq0.46\) s. There \(|\mathcal Q|\) peaks at \(3.5\times10^{-36}\), an outgoing amplitude of \(1.5\times10^{-11}\) of the incident one.
80: - After the pulse has left, the star rings in radial p modes, with dominant periods of 44–59 s and an rms readout of \(8.1\times10^{-45}\).
81: 
82: **Imported from prior work.** Only the endpoint is cross-checked against the coupled calculation. The model is linear, adiabatic and Newtonian. The photosphere, whose thermal times are at most 0.4 s (about 1 s with ionization energy), is not adiabatic during the ringing.
83: 
84: **Conjectural.** The endpoint readout is therefore the retarded monopole of a transient redistribution, Equation (\ref{eq:wd-window}), and its value is tied to the protocol. It is not a static charge of the displaced configuration.
85: 
86: **Proven.** Consider a discrete linear system with positive-definite mass and stiffness matrices, driven by a force of finite duration. After the force ends, the response is a finite sum of modes of nonzero frequency. Its time average vanishes, and it tends to zero if every mode is positively damped. **Imported from prior work.** The radial model's lowest eigenvalue is \(\omega_0^2=1.25\times10^{-3}\) s\(^{-2}\), a period of 178 s. **Conjectural.** Suppose the star has no neutral modes and all its modes are damped, including the thermal (entropy) modes that the adiabatic model omits; we made no non-adiabatic stability calculation. Then the pulse-induced change of \(a_i\) vanishes at first order in \(\eta\). A change of order \(\eta^2\) from absorbed energy remains.
87: 
88: The endpoint value is subject to the following corrections.
89: 
90: - **Proven.** In the Newtonian radial channel, self-gravity feedback changes the displacement over \(t_e\) by at most a fraction \(2\pi G\rho_{\max}t_e^2=4.5\times10^{-18}\), where \(\rho_{\max}\) is the largest \(\rho_0\) in the evolved layers. **Conjectural.** With post-Newtonian terms neglected, the once-returned GR solution differs from the self-consistent one at this order.
91: - **Conjectural.** Curvature backscatter changes the flat-space readout at order \(m_{\rm wd}/R_d=4.2\times10^{-6}\). This is an order estimate, not a bound.
92: - **Proven.** Because \(\alpha_s\) is linear in \(\varphi\), the second-order force and source terms are at most \(|\delta\varphi|/\varphi_0=1.0\times10^{-27}\) of the first-order ones. This compares those terms only; it does not bound the full readout error.
93: - **Proven.** Invariance under \(\varphi\to-\varphi\) makes \(\mathcal Q/\eta\), at first order in \(\eta\), even in \(\varphi_\infty\). **Conjectural.** Since the force and the readout weight are both proportional to \(\alpha_s(\varphi_0)\), the dynamic part scales as \(\varphi_\infty^2\) at leading order in the weak background field. The exponent of the \(10^{-8}\) direct part is not fixed.
94: - **Imported from prior work.** The ADM mass differs from the mass at the readout radius by a relative \(6.8\times10^{-11}\).
95: 
96: **Imported from prior work.** The photosphere sets the magnitude of the readout.
97: 
98: - The kernel centroid lies 363 km below the \(P_{\rm gas}=1\) dyn cm\(^{-2}\) cut, at gray optical depth 1.06.
99: - The kernel is nearly single-signed. No relative change of the face densities below 99.87% in the maximum norm reverses the sign, and the tested smooth structure families keep it.
100: - A spherical gray envelope rebuilt with the same equation of state and tables reproduces the declared density with a kernel-weighted deviation of 0.25% (maximum 0.38%).
101: - At fixed mass, and with the pulse kernel of the declared geometry, varying \(\log g\) or \(T_{\rm eff}\) alone within Kaplan's 1\(\sigma\) ranges scales \(|\mathcal Q(t_e)|\) by 1.6–6.2 (3.2 at the central values).
102: - An LTE non-gray correction multiplies this by a further 1.9–2.4 over those 1\(\sigma\) cases (2.1 at the central values). It uses H and He continuum opacity whose Rosseland mean is 0.55–0.74 of the tables, is plane-parallel, and is converged to \(5.6\times10^{-5}\) in flux for \(\tau_R\le100\).
103: 
104: **Conjectural.** These magnitudes only show how strongly the protocol readout depends on surface gravity; no conclusion below uses them.
105: 
106: **Imported from prior work.** At orbital timescales, the white dwarf's radial (\(l=0\)) restoring times, below 180 s, are far shorter than the lags \(\tau_\chi=2\)–500 d scanned in Section 5.
107: 
108: - **Imported from prior work.** *Radial.* The lowest radial mode has a period of 178 s, and the acoustic cutoff periods of the charge layers are 28–47 s. At the inner orbit the in-phase correction is \((\omega/\omega_0)^2=1.6\times10^{-6}\). At equal amplitude, a drive longer than the star exerts a monopole force about \(3\times10^{-8}\) of the pulse's through the \(\varphi_0'\) term. **Conjectural.** Including the change of the self-coupling, a relative change of the effective gravity of at most 0.048 per unit uniform scalar shift, raises this to order \(10^{-7}\).
109: - **Proven.** *Tidal.* On a static spherical background, linear response couples \(a_i\) only to the \(l=0\) part of the drive, so tides do not enter it at linear order.
110: - **Imported from prior work.** *Tidal magnitudes.* The equilibrium tide has apsidal constant \(k_2=3.3\times10^{-4}\) and relative tidal force \(9.1\times10^{-12}\); its apsidal rate is \(1.5\times10^{-5}\) of the 1PN rate.
111: - **Conjectural.** *Second order and resonance.* At second order the tide has an \(l=0\) part. Treating the squared tidal parameter as a uniform change of the effective gravity gives a modulation of \(a_i\) of \(3.7\times10^{-19}\) at \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\) for the equilibrium tide. A one-way radiative damping-depth estimate for the dynamical tide (\(8.9\times10^6\)) uses a weak-damping formula outside its validity, so discrete g-mode resonances, which could raise this, are not excluded.
112: - **Imported from prior work.** *Dissipative.* The adiabatic static structural susceptibility \(\mathcal S_{\rm struct}\) is the change of \(a_i\) per uniform scalar shift from hydrostatic readjustment. We find \(|\mathcal S_{\rm struct}|\le8.84\times10^{-9}\) at \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\), at most \(2.21\times10^{-9}\) of \(|\beta_s|\), scaling as \(\varphi_\infty^2\). The thermal time of the overlying layers is only 4 s at the envelope base, 624 km deep (up to 2.5 times longer with ionization energy).
113: - **Conjectural.** *Deeper layers.* Thermal times grow inward to the stellar cooling time, so by continuity some deeper layer has a thermal time comparable to the orbital period. Its depth was not located.
114: - **Conjectural.** *Lag bound.* Two assumptions, neither computed, give a lag bound: the thermally relaxing part of the structural response is no larger than \(\mathcal S_{\rm struct}\), and it relaxes with a single time constant (Debye form). The lagged part is then at most \(|\mathcal S_{\rm struct}|/2\) per unit drive. Resonant, strongly overdamped or mixed-sign multi-relaxation dynamics fall outside this bound.
115: 
116: **Conjectural.** For J0337 we compare scales only. A change \(\delta a_i\) modulates the pulsar–inner pair factor by \(a_p\,\delta a_i\) and the inner–outer pair factor by \(a_o\,\delta a_i\), and leaves the pulsar–outer pair unchanged. This is not the common modulation of the Section 4.3 template, so the coefficient envelopes of Table 1 do not translate into it (Section 5.4). **Imported from prior work.** To leading order in the eccentricities, the orbital modulation of the companions' scalar field at the white dwarf obeys
117: 
118: \begin{equation}
119: \delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\le3.08\times10^{-10}|a_p|+2.04\times10^{-10}|a_o|,
120: \label{eq:wd-modulation}
121: \end{equation}
122: 
123: where the first term comes from the inner eccentricity and the second from the outer eccentricity and the inner-orbit motion. The charge responds in two ways.
124: 
125: - **Proven.** *Instantaneous.* In the weak-field limit \(a_i=\alpha_s(\varphi)\) at the white dwarf, so \(\delta a_i=\beta_s\,\delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\) follows the field without lag. By Equation (\ref{eq:wd-modulation}) it is at most \(1.24\times10^{-9}|a_p|+8.2\times10^{-10}|a_o|\). It is set by the static coefficient \(\beta_s\) and falls under the feedback-stiffness condition that Section 4.3 places on responsive companions.
126: - **Conjectural.** *Structural.* The adiabatic structural part \(\delta a_i^{\rm struct}=\mathcal S_{\rm struct}\,\delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\) is in phase, up to the correction \((\omega/\omega_0)^2\). Under the two assumptions of the lag bound, the thermally relaxing part is at most \(|\mathcal S_{\rm struct}|\,\delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\), and at most half of it lags the drive.
127: 
128: **Imported from prior work.** The Cassini measurement \(\gamma-1=(2.1\pm2.3)\times10^{-5}\) \cite{bertotti2003cassini}, with \(\gamma-1=-2\alpha_0^2/(1+\alpha_0^2)\) \cite{damour1992tensor} taken at its 2\(\sigma\) lower limit, gives \(|\alpha_0|\le3.54\times10^{-3}\), or \(\varphi_\infty\le8.84\times10^{-4}\) for \(\beta_s=-4\). The declared \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\) lies outside this range, so we rescale \(\mathcal S_{\rm struct}\) as \(\varphi_\infty^2\). **Conjectural.** With \(|a_o|\simeq|\alpha_0|\) and \(|a_p|\le1\), the scale \(|\mathcal S_{\rm struct}|\,\delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\) of the in-phase and, under the two assumptions, the relaxing structural modulation is \(2.13\times10^{-18}\) for the pulsar–inner pair factor and \(7.53\times10^{-21}\) for the inner–outer pair factor. These lie at least eight orders of magnitude below the stored Section 5 scales at \(\tau_\chi=2\) d, the shortest stored lag, where the Table 1 envelopes are smallest:
129: 
130: - \(2.8\times10^{-10}\) (K=1, truncated)
131: - \(4.1\times10^{-10}\) (physical-drive endpoint)
132: - \(1.7\times10^{-9}\) (K=10, truncated)
133: - \(3.5\times10^{-9}\) (K=10, full)
134: - \(1.57\times10^{-7}\) (K\(\approx\)934)
135: 
136: **Conjectural.** We did not compute the timing response of these two non-common channels, including the nuisance projection. Reading the comparison as a sensitivity statement would require their timing sensitivity to lie within about eight orders of magnitude of the common template's, which was not established. The outer white dwarf's own internal states were not computed.
137: 
138: **Conjectural.** In summary, a scalar pulse leaves in the displaced matter of the inner white dwarf a transient retarded monopole signal, with no first-order permanent charge if all its modes are damped. At orbital timescales the charge follows the field instantaneously through \(\beta_s\), and the displacement state adds a small structural term whose lagged part is bounded only under the assumed single-relaxation form. The calculation therefore neither establishes the orbital-timescale internal state that the Section 3 benchmark requires nor shows that none exists. The remaining assumptions are:
139: 
140: - linear response;
141: - a Newtonian radial model with a flat-exterior readout;
142: - no neutral modes and positive damping of all modes;
143: - neglect of interior Born scattering, where the core compactness is about \(10^{-4}\);
144: - a thermal relaxation strength bounded by \(\mathcal S_{\rm struct}\), with a single-relaxation form;
145: - the unfixed \(\varphi_\infty\) exponent of the \(10^{-8}\) direct part;
146: - a non-gray correction with simplified LTE continuum opacity and a fixed pulse kernel;
147: - a slowly rotating white dwarf;
148: - a mixed hydrogen–helium envelope, whereas the observed star has a hydrogen (DA) atmosphere \cite{kaplan2014j0337}.
149: 
